{{- /* The text `helm install` prints. It lives in a named template so that the tests can render it. */ -}}
{{- define "molesignal.notes" -}}
{{- $ns := .Release.Namespace -}}
{{- $front := include "molesignal.frontService" . -}}
{{- $httpPort := include "molesignal.port" (dict "ctx" . "name" "http") -}}
MoleSignal {{ .Chart.AppVersion }} was installed as release "{{ .Release.Name }}" in {{ .Values.mode }} mode.

1. Wait for the pods to become ready (the first start pulls images and replays the
   write-ahead log, which can take a minute or two):

     kubectl --namespace {{ $ns }} get pods --selector app.kubernetes.io/instance={{ .Release.Name }} --watch

   Then check the API end to end:

     helm test {{ .Release.Name }} --namespace {{ $ns }}

2. Open the UI:
{{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }}
{{- range .Values.ingress.hosts }}

     {{ ternary "https" "http" (not (empty $.Values.ingress.tls)) }}://{{ .host }}
{{- end }}

   Behind an ingress every user counts as the controller's address until you list its pods as
   trusted proxies: in the `_sys` organization, Settings > Client IP recognition.
{{- else }}

     kubectl --namespace {{ $ns }} port-forward svc/{{ $front }} {{ $httpPort }}:{{ $httpPort }}

   and browse to http://127.0.0.1:{{ $httpPort }}. To expose it, set `ingress.enabled`.
{{- end }}

3. Send telemetry. Inside the cluster, OTLP over gRPC goes to

     {{ include "molesignal.otlpService" . }}.{{ $ns }}.svc.cluster.local:{{ include "molesignal.port" (dict "ctx" . "name" "otlp-grpc") }}

   and the HTTP API and OTLP over HTTP are on port {{ $httpPort }} of {{ $front }}.
{{- if or .Values.postgresql.enabled .Values.rustfs.enabled }}

{{ "!! " }}Evaluation setup: the bundled
{{- if and .Values.postgresql.enabled .Values.rustfs.enabled }} PostgreSQL and RustFS are{{ else if .Values.postgresql.enabled }} PostgreSQL is{{ else }} RustFS is{{ end }} one instance without backups or
   failover. For production, turn {{ if and .Values.postgresql.enabled .Values.rustfs.enabled }}them{{ else }}it{{ end }} off and use `externalDatabase` / `objectStore`
   (see examples/production.yaml in the chart repository).
{{- end }}

{{ "!! " }}Back up the field-level encryption key. Without it, encrypted fields cannot be read
   again, even from a restored database:

     kubectl --namespace {{ $ns }} get secret {{ include "molesignal.secretName" . }} \
       --output jsonpath='{.data.{{ .Values.secrets.keys.cipherKey }}}' | base64 --decode

{{- if and .Values.rustfs.enabled .Values.rustfs.console.enabled }}

The RustFS console (S3 browser) is on port 9001 of {{ include "molesignal.componentName" (dict "ctx" . "component" "rustfs") }}:

     kubectl --namespace {{ $ns }} port-forward svc/{{ include "molesignal.componentName" (dict "ctx" . "component" "rustfs") }} 9001:9001

   Sign in with the credentials in secret {{ include "molesignal.rustfs.secretName" . }}.
{{- end }}
{{- if and (not .Values.secrets.cipherKey) (not .Values.secrets.existingSecret) }}

Secrets that you did not set were generated by the chart and are read back from the
cluster on every upgrade. If you render the chart with `helm template` (Argo CD, for
example) they cannot be read back and would change on every sync: set
`secrets.cipherKey`, `secrets.clusterToken` and the database / object store credentials
explicitly, or point `secrets.existingSecret` at a Secret you manage.
{{- end }}
{{- end -}}
